<section
  id='case-petart-logotype'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Pet Art With Heart — Logo Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Graphic design case study for a hand-drawn pet portrait identity, developed from an original pencil sketch into a warm and flexible logo system.'
  data-case='petart'
  aria-labelledby='case-petart-logotype-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-petart-logotype-title'>Pet Art With Heart</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A hand-drawn identity that brings together pet portraiture, fine art, and the emotional
          connection people have with their animals.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand design and illustration</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent real-world brand</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Status</dt>
            <dd>Ongoing</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Pencil and paper, Adobe Illustrator</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Flexible brand identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='petart-logotype'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Pet Art With Heart sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='petart'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Pet Art With Heart is my own pet portrait business, so the identity needed to feel personal
        while still looking established and professional. I wanted one recognisable symbol that
        could connect pets, art, and the meaning behind the word “heart.” It also needed to work
        across the website, social media, profile images, and printed material without losing its
        character.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The idea included several visual elements: a dog, a cat, a heart, and an artist's
            palette. Combining all of them too literally would have made the logo complicated and
            difficult to recognise at a smaller size. The main challenge was finding a simple form
            that communicated the idea without feeling like a collection of separate symbols.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Connect pet companionship and artistic work within one symbol
            </li>
            <li>
              Create something emotional without making it overly playful
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep the character of the original drawing during vector refinement
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/logo-sketch-petart-900.webp'
            data-src='logo-sketch-petart-900.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='900'
            height='597'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart pencil sketch beside the refined vector symbol of a dog and cat forming a heart and artist palette'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            The original pencil sketch beside the simplified vector symbol
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I used the curved silhouettes of the dog and cat to form a heart through their shared
              negative space instead of adding a separate heart shape.
            </li>
            <li>
              The palette holes are integrated into the cat's body. This keeps the reference to
              painting visible without adding another standalone object.
            </li>
            <li>
              I paired the organic symbol with a classic serif wordmark to balance the warmth of the
              illustration with a more established and trustworthy feeling.
            </li>
            <li>
              I created horizontal, vertical, icon-only, monochrome, and reversed versions so the
              identity could adapt to different formats and backgrounds.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/design-petartlogosuite-600.webp'
            data-src='design-petartlogosuite-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='423'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Pet Art With Heart logo suite showing primary, secondary, icon, vertical, monochrome, and reversed variations'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Logo variations prepared for websites, social media, and monochrome use
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished identity connects my fine-art work with a practical brand system. The icon is
        detailed enough to feel personal but simple enough to remain recognisable as a small profile
        image. The different lockups also give me flexibility when adding the logo to new formats.
        This project showed me how an expressive pencil sketch can keep its original personality
        after being simplified and prepared for real-world use.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>